"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,656\nSpotting the next myth\n\n4\n00:00:12,456 --> 00:00:17,066\nOver this series, we have looked at eleven popular ideas about the brain.\n\n5\n00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:19,411\nMost began with something real.\n\n6\n00:00:19,719 --> 00:00:20,897\nMost were stretched.\n\n7\n00:00:21,138 --> 00:00:23,015\nSo how do you spot the next one?\n\n8\n00:00:23,185 --> 00:00:25,377\nStart with a warning about ourselves.\n\n9\n00:00:25,738 --> 00:00:32,872\nIn 2008, Deena Weisberg and colleagues gave people good and bad explanations of psychology findings.\n\n10\n00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:39,460\nAdding irrelevant brain language made bad explanations seem more satisfying to non experts.\n\n11\n00:00:39,879 --> 00:00:43,661\nA 2024 replication found the effect real,\n\n12\n00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:44,878\nbut smaller.\n\n13\n00:00:45,048 --> 00:00:47,391\nSo, five questions.\n\n14\n00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:50,349\nOne: who is making the claim,\n\n15\n00:00:50,696 --> 00:00:51,246\nand are they selling something?\n\n16\n00:00:51,497 --> 00:00:53,937\nTwo: was it tested against a comparison group,\n\n17\n00:00:53,957 --> 00:00:55,538\nnot just before and after?\n\n18\n00:00:55,708 --> 00:00:58,582\nThree: did it change the outcome you care about,\n\n19\n00:00:58,602 --> 00:01:01,724\nlike learning, or just a game score or a feeling?\n\n20\n00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:04,668\nFour: has anyone else replicated it?\n\n21\n00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:06,951\nFive: take out the brain language.\n\n22\n00:01:07,294 --> 00:01:09,028\nDoes the claim still make sense,\n\n23\n00:01:09,098 --> 00:01:10,926\nand is there still evidence for it?\n\n24\n00:01:11,096 --> 00:01:13,183\nRun the myths through these questions,\n\n25\n00:01:13,203 --> 00:01:14,432\nand most fail fast.\n\n26\n00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:17,117\nLearning styles fails question two.\n\n27\n00:01:17,438 --> 00:01:19,613\nBrain games fail question three.\n\n28\n00:01:19,972 --> 00:01:22,410\nThe Mozart effect fails question four.\n\n29\n00:01:22,768 --> 00:01:26,100\nBrain Gym fails question one and question five.\n\n30\n00:01:26,270 --> 00:01:27,246\nSome caution.\n\n31\n00:01:27,617 --> 00:01:29,812\nSkepticism is not cynicism.\n\n32\n00:01:30,139 --> 00:01:33,901\nReal findings exist, and this series has relied on them.\n\n33\n00:01:34,349 --> 00:01:36,543\nThe aim is not to doubt everything,\n\n34\n00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:39,623\nbut to ask for the evidence that matches the claim.\n\n35\n00:01:39,793 --> 00:01:41,019\nDo this instead.\n\n36\n00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:44,809\nKeep these five questions next to any new teaching idea.\n\n37\n00:01:45,121 --> 00:01:48,560\nShare your checking process with students,\n\n38\n00:01:48,944 --> 00:01:50,883\nso they learn it too.\n\n39\n00:01:51,146 --> 00:01:54,303\nOne tip to share with students: be most careful when a claim sounds most scientific.\n\n40\n00:01:54,473 --> 00:01:56,716\nCuriosity about the brain is good.\n\n41\n00:01:57,048 --> 00:01:59,482\nPair it with a habit of asking how we know.\n\n42\n00:01:59,783 --> 00:02:01,840\nThanks for watching series six.\n\n"